    "facts": "By the impugned judgment and order of acquittal, learned Magistrate acquitted the respondents of offences under Section 7(i) read with Section 2(ia)(f) and under Section 7(v) read with Rule 48B punishable under Section 16(1)(a)(ii) of the Prevention of the Food Adulteration Act, 1954.\nDuring the pendency of the present appeal, respondent No.2-Varajlal Jiwanbhai Popat, accused No.2, expired and, therefore, as per order dated 8.4.2019, the appeal against him was ordered to be disposed of as abated.\nHe divided the sample in 3 equal parts.\nThe sample of Suji/Rava was sent to Public Analyst who reported that said sample does not conform standard of \"Semolina\" and was \"Insect Infested\".\n8. In the present case, the accused purchased Suji/Rava from Vijaykimar & Co. at Amravati on 16.8.1994.\nWhereas, the complainant collected the sample on 27.9.1994.\nState Of Maharashtra At The Instance Of ... vs Nitin Varajlal Popat & Anor on 14 June, 2019 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/18390196/\nThe report does not state as to when the sample was opened and analyzed.\n",
    "ANALYSIS": "The Appellate Court can always upset order of acquittal if it is noticed by the Appellate Court that approach on the part of learned Judge below is perverse and appreciation of evidence is not in accordance with law.\nThere is no evidence to show that in which condition Public Analyst had kept the sample or when he analyzed the same.\n",
